id: k8s.daemon-set
kind: kubernetes
title: DaemonSet
aliases: daemonset, one pod per node
tags: workloads, node agents
questions:
  - how do you run an agent on every node?
invariant: Exactly one Pod per eligible node, including nodes added later.
answer_30s: A DaemonSet places one Pod on every node matching its selector, and nodes that join later automatically get that Pod.
answer_2m: |
  The controller watches Nodes as well as Pods, so no scaling action is needed
  when a node joins. Tolerations are how node-specific agents are allowed onto
  tainted nodes, for example control-plane nodes. Typical uses: log shippers,
  metric agents, CNI and CSI node plugins, kube-proxy.
when_to_use:
  - per-node agents and node-level device plugins
avoid_when:
  - you simply want N copies of a service
commands:
  - kubectl get daemonset -A => per-node agents and how many nodes are covered
  - kubectl describe daemonset -n kube-system <agent> => which nodes were skipped and why
failure_modes:
  - no tolerations, so tainted nodes are silently uncovered
  - resource requests too high for small nodes, leaving them uncovered
related:
  - k8s.node
  - k8s.deployment
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/daemonset/
